1
00:00:00,326 --> 00:00:04,812
In August nineteen forty-five, Japan
still had thousands of hidden warplanes.

2
00:00:05,617 --> 00:00:08,396
It still had warships, sailors,
and harbors full of mines.

3
00:00:09,168 --> 00:00:11,378
Two years later, almost
all of it was gone.

4
00:00:12,130 --> 00:00:16,218
But in nineteen fifty, a few of its old
ships slipped out in secret, heading for

5
00:00:16,258 --> 00:00:17,258
a war.

6
00:00:17,602 --> 00:00:19,778
So what really happened
to Japan's war machine?

7
00:00:20,586 --> 00:00:24,942
On September second, nineteen forty-five,
Japan signed the surrender aboard the

8
00:00:24,982 --> 00:00:27,754
battleship USS Missouri in Tokyo Bay.

9
00:00:28,768 --> 00:00:30,935
The Allies' first demand was simple.

10
00:00:31,034 --> 00:00:33,864
Nothing Japanese would
ever fly into battle again.

11
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:38,678
Japan's leaders had been saving those
hidden planes for one final battle.

12
00:00:39,537 --> 00:00:42,215
They expected an invasion
of the home islands.

13
00:00:42,291 --> 00:00:46,063
Many of the planes were meant to be
flown straight into the invasion fleet.

14
00:00:46,998 --> 00:00:50,993
Across Japan, ground crews were now
ordered to take the propellers off their

15
00:00:51,033 --> 00:00:52,033
own aircraft.

16
00:00:52,829 --> 00:00:56,664
Fighters that had been fueled for that
last battle sat on the grass like birds

17
00:00:56,704 --> 00:00:58,110
with their wings clipped.

18
00:00:58,979 --> 00:01:02,422
Many of the young pilots had trained
for missions they were never meant to

19
00:01:02,462 --> 00:01:03,462
return from.

20
00:01:04,154 --> 00:01:07,627
Now they watched the planes they would
have died in being dragged into piles.

21
00:01:08,451 --> 00:01:11,313
But the Americans did not
destroy everything at once.

22
00:01:12,053 --> 00:01:14,663
They wanted to know exactly
how these machines were built.

23
00:01:15,395 --> 00:01:19,951
So intelligence teams picked out about
four examples of each new aircraft type.

24
00:01:20,727 --> 00:01:24,710
Those planes were crated, loaded onto
ships, and sent across the Pacific to

25
00:01:24,750 --> 00:01:25,750
be tested.

26
00:01:26,225 --> 00:01:28,883
A few of them still sit
in American museums today.

27
00:01:29,651 --> 00:01:32,018
The rest were burned,
crushed, or cut up for scrap.

28
00:01:32,704 --> 00:01:35,352
The work dragged on well
into nineteen forty-seven.

29
00:01:36,178 --> 00:01:37,715
Planes were easy to destroy, though.

30
00:01:38,417 --> 00:01:41,856
Ships were a different problem,
because Japan desperately needed them.

31
00:01:42,727 --> 00:01:46,958
When the war ended, more than six million
Japanese were stranded far from home.

32
00:01:47,819 --> 00:01:50,680
About half were soldiers, and
the other half were civilians.

33
00:01:51,453 --> 00:01:55,491
They were scattered from the cold plains
of Manchuria to the jungles of New Guinea.

34
00:01:56,325 --> 00:01:58,229
Many of them were sick and hungry.

35
00:01:58,333 --> 00:02:02,062
They waited on beaches and in camps
with nothing but a name on a list.

36
00:02:03,003 --> 00:02:05,645
So the defeated navy was
given one last mission.

37
00:02:06,415 --> 00:02:10,307
Its surviving warships were stripped
of their guns and turned into ferries.

38
00:02:11,112 --> 00:02:12,868
Even aircraft carriers joined in.

39
00:02:13,572 --> 00:02:16,417
The carrier Katsuragi had been
finished too late to fight.

40
00:02:17,242 --> 00:02:19,617
She never sent a single
squadron into battle.

41
00:02:20,377 --> 00:02:24,469
Now her empty hangars were filled with
exhausted men coming home from places

42
00:02:24,509 --> 00:02:25,509
like Rabaul.

43
00:02:26,380 --> 00:02:29,164
The old carrier Hosho
became a passenger ship too.

44
00:02:29,912 --> 00:02:33,267
She was one of the first ships in the
world designed as a carrier from the

45
00:02:33,307 --> 00:02:34,307
keel up.

46
00:02:34,658 --> 00:02:38,658
For a soldier who had not seen Japan in
years, the first view of home came from

47
00:02:38,698 --> 00:02:39,698
a warship.

48
00:02:40,221 --> 00:02:42,107
It was a warship that had lost its war.

49
00:02:42,888 --> 00:02:44,718
But this mission could not last forever.

50
00:02:45,448 --> 00:02:49,504
By the end of nineteen forty-six,
Katsuragi was being cut up for scrap.

51
00:02:50,271 --> 00:02:54,102
Once the ferries were no longer needed,
the winners had to decide who would keep

52
00:02:54,142 --> 00:02:55,142
the rest.

53
00:02:55,714 --> 00:03:00,199
Four Allied powers wanted a share: the
United States, Britain, China, and the

54
00:03:00,239 --> 00:03:01,239
Soviet Union.

55
00:03:01,852 --> 00:03:06,006
China asked for special treatment, because
its own navy had been almost wiped out.

56
00:03:06,935 --> 00:03:09,691
The solution sounds
almost like a game show.

57
00:03:09,799 --> 00:03:13,244
The remaining destroyers and smaller
warships were split into four

58
00:03:13,284 --> 00:03:14,284
equal groups.

59
00:03:15,101 --> 00:03:17,979
Each country then drew lots to
see which group it would receive.

60
00:03:18,856 --> 00:03:21,225
One of those ships was
the destroyer Yukikaze.

61
00:03:21,935 --> 00:03:24,967
She had fought in some of the
biggest naval battles of the war.

62
00:03:25,735 --> 00:03:27,583
Somehow, she survived every one of them.

63
00:03:28,306 --> 00:03:32,663
Out of eighty-two Japanese destroyers
built before the war, she was one of only

64
00:03:32,703 --> 00:03:33,876
three still afloat.

65
00:03:34,758 --> 00:03:39,184
In July nineteen forty-seven, she was
handed over to China and given a new name,

66
00:03:39,315 --> 00:03:40,315
Tan Yang.

67
00:03:40,778 --> 00:03:43,142
Her old enemy now flew
its flag over her deck.

68
00:03:43,943 --> 00:03:47,578
Another survivor, the destroyer
Hibiki, went to the Soviet Union.

69
00:03:48,371 --> 00:03:51,061
The Soviets renamed her
Verniy, which means faithful.

70
00:03:51,814 --> 00:03:53,951
She was faithful to a new owner now.

71
00:03:54,029 --> 00:03:57,831
Most of the ships that went to America
and Britain had a shorter future.

72
00:03:58,770 --> 00:04:01,522
They were not needed, so most
of them were simply scrapped.

73
00:04:02,358 --> 00:04:04,515
But some ships were too
famous to give away.

74
00:04:05,247 --> 00:04:07,768
For those, the Americans
had a very different plan.

75
00:04:08,612 --> 00:04:12,957
The battleship Nagato had once been the
flagship of the Combined Fleet, Japan's

76
00:04:12,997 --> 00:04:14,195
main battle fleet.

77
00:04:15,064 --> 00:04:18,732
Admiral Yamamoto had commanded from
her decks in the first days of the

78
00:04:18,772 --> 00:04:19,772
Pacific war.

79
00:04:20,520 --> 00:04:24,215
By the end of the war, she was the
last Japanese battleship still afloat.

80
00:04:25,202 --> 00:04:28,653
An American crew took her over
and sailed her across the Pacific.

81
00:04:29,474 --> 00:04:33,099
Her destination was a tiny ring
of islands called Bikini Atoll.

82
00:04:33,927 --> 00:04:36,349
There, she became a target
for atomic bomb tests.

83
00:04:37,055 --> 00:04:41,474
On July first, nineteen forty-six, the
first bomb exploded in the air above the

84
00:04:41,514 --> 00:04:42,514
anchored fleet.

85
00:04:43,304 --> 00:04:45,607
Nagato survived with little damage.

86
00:04:45,651 --> 00:04:48,704
Three weeks later, a second
bomb exploded underwater.

87
00:04:49,583 --> 00:04:52,247
The old battleship stayed
afloat for four more days.

88
00:04:52,987 --> 00:04:56,697
Then, on the night of July twenty-ninth,
she slowly rolled over and sank.

89
00:04:57,500 --> 00:05:00,176
Today she lies upside down
on the bottom of the lagoon.

90
00:05:01,010 --> 00:05:03,414
So the planes were burned,
and the big ships were gone.

91
00:05:04,187 --> 00:05:07,655
But one part of the war machine was
still out there, waiting in the water.

92
00:05:08,566 --> 00:05:12,368
During the war, Japan had laid tens
of thousands of mines to protect its

93
00:05:12,408 --> 00:05:13,408
own harbors.

94
00:05:14,138 --> 00:05:17,724
American bombers dropped thousands
more to strangle Japan's shipping.

95
00:05:18,517 --> 00:05:20,828
The mines did not know the war was over.

96
00:05:20,921 --> 00:05:25,339
Fishing boats, ferries and cargo ships
kept hitting them, and people kept dying.

97
00:05:26,367 --> 00:05:27,779
Somebody had to clear the water.

98
00:05:28,474 --> 00:05:32,195
Therefore the Allies kept one small
piece of the Japanese navy alive:

99
00:05:33,003 --> 00:05:34,150
its minesweepers.

100
00:05:34,240 --> 00:05:37,884
Many of the crews were the same men
who had served in the Imperial Navy.

101
00:05:38,750 --> 00:05:42,049
They went out day after day,
dragging cables through cold water.

102
00:05:42,842 --> 00:05:46,311
Every sailor knew the next mine could
go off right under his own hull.

103
00:05:47,148 --> 00:05:49,887
Their work was slow, dangerous,
and almost invisible.

104
00:05:50,673 --> 00:05:54,454
It was the only part of Japan's war
machine that never stopped working.

105
00:05:55,338 --> 00:05:57,801
But soon, Japan would face
a much bigger question.

106
00:05:58,634 --> 00:06:02,793
In May nineteen forty-seven, Japan's
new constitution came into force.

107
00:06:03,587 --> 00:06:06,596
Its ninth article said that
Japan gave up war forever.

108
00:06:07,383 --> 00:06:11,115
It also said that Japan would never
keep land, sea, or air forces.

109
00:06:11,917 --> 00:06:15,902
For the first time in its modern
history, Japan had no military at all.

110
00:06:16,798 --> 00:06:21,240
Then, on June twenty-fifth, nineteen
fifty, North Korea invaded South Korea.

111
00:06:22,075 --> 00:06:25,687
The American troops guarding Japan
were rushed across the sea to fight.

112
00:06:26,535 --> 00:06:29,152
Suddenly, Japan itself
was almost undefended.

113
00:06:29,955 --> 00:06:34,035
Within weeks, the occupation authorities
ordered a new force called the National

114
00:06:34,075 --> 00:06:37,066
Police Reserve, with
seventy-five thousand men.

115
00:06:38,030 --> 00:06:42,164
It was called a police force, but it was
armed and organized much like an army.

116
00:06:43,006 --> 00:06:45,515
On land, Japan now had an answer of sorts.

117
00:06:45,598 --> 00:06:47,145
The sea was another story.

118
00:06:48,021 --> 00:06:52,945
In October nineteen fifty, United Nations
forces planned a landing at Wonsan, a port

119
00:06:52,985 --> 00:06:54,818
on North Korea's east coast.

120
00:06:55,720 --> 00:06:58,385
But the harbor was full
of Soviet-made mines.

121
00:06:58,434 --> 00:07:02,062
The Americans did not have enough
minesweepers to clear them in time.

122
00:07:03,085 --> 00:07:06,957
So on October second, Rear Admiral
Arleigh Burke made a quiet request.

123
00:07:07,754 --> 00:07:12,943
He asked Takeo Okubo, the head of Japan's
new Maritime Safety Agency, to send

124
00:07:12,983 --> 00:07:14,362
minesweepers to Korea.

125
00:07:15,299 --> 00:07:19,226
Okubo refused at first, because the
new constitution seemed to forbid it.

126
00:07:20,113 --> 00:07:22,988
He took the question to Prime
Minister Shigeru Yoshida.

127
00:07:23,046 --> 00:07:24,323
Yoshida said yes.

128
00:07:25,166 --> 00:07:29,343
On October sixth, sixteen Japanese
minesweepers carrying about twelve hundred

129
00:07:29,383 --> 00:07:32,270
men left the port of
Shimonoseki for Korea.

130
00:07:33,239 --> 00:07:37,088
Many of these small ships had been built
for the Imperial Navy during the war.

131
00:07:37,923 --> 00:07:40,838
Five years earlier, these
sailors had been America's enemy.

132
00:07:41,613 --> 00:07:44,476
Now they were sweeping mines
in front of an American fleet.

133
00:07:45,281 --> 00:07:48,677
Off Wonsan, minesweeper M S
fourteen struck a mine and sank.

134
00:07:49,466 --> 00:07:52,053
One man was killed, and
eighteen were injured.

135
00:07:52,184 --> 00:07:56,944
A month later, an American army tugboat
with a Japanese crew hit another mine.

136
00:07:57,933 --> 00:08:00,127
Twenty-two of its
twenty-seven crewmen died.

137
00:08:00,908 --> 00:08:02,039
There was a bitter twist.

138
00:08:02,641 --> 00:08:06,547
While the mines were being cleared, South
Korean troops had already taken Wonsan

139
00:08:06,587 --> 00:08:07,587
by land.

140
00:08:08,213 --> 00:08:12,018
When the American landing finally
came, the beach was already secure.

141
00:08:12,837 --> 00:08:15,437
But the men who died clearing
the way did not come back.

142
00:08:16,249 --> 00:08:18,077
Their families were told almost nothing.

143
00:08:18,783 --> 00:08:23,014
The whole operation stayed top secret
for twenty-nine years, and no medals were

144
00:08:23,054 --> 00:08:24,054
ever given.

145
00:08:24,732 --> 00:08:26,907
So what really happened
to Japan's war machine?

146
00:08:27,643 --> 00:08:30,067
Its planes were stripped of
their propellers and burned.

147
00:08:30,805 --> 00:08:33,339
Its warships carried
millions of people home.

148
00:08:33,458 --> 00:08:37,475
After that, they were raffled off,
scrapped, or sunk by atomic bombs.

149
00:08:38,410 --> 00:08:41,812
But its smallest ships, and the men
who sailed them, never really stopped.

150
00:08:42,628 --> 00:08:46,905
Five years after the surrender, they were
back in a war zone, sweeping mines for

151
00:08:46,945 --> 00:08:47,971
their former enemy.

152
00:08:48,854 --> 00:08:53,429
In nineteen fifty-four, Japan created
its Maritime Self-Defense Force, and the

153
00:08:53,469 --> 00:08:55,528
minesweeping crews became part of it.

154
00:08:56,503 --> 00:08:59,000
The war machine had been
taken apart, piece by piece.

155
00:08:59,728 --> 00:09:02,015
But a few of its pieces
had simply been waiting.

156
00:09:02,817 --> 00:09:06,764
Japan was not the only defeated
navy that the winners divided up.

157
00:09:07,504 --> 00:09:11,660
Italy's battleships were handed out too,
and one of them exploded in a Soviet

158
00:09:11,700 --> 00:09:13,532
harbor ten years after the war.

159
00:09:14,526 --> 00:09:18,328
That mystery is our next story, so
subscribe if you want to find out what

160
00:09:18,368 --> 00:09:19,368
really sank her.

